WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.316 --> 00:00:02.386
- [Narrator] Solitaire is
one of the most popular

00:00:02.386 --> 00:00:04.695
computer games of all time,

00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:07.548
and we went to find
the guy who created it.

00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:08.545
(tires screech)

00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:11.053
(doorbell rings)

00:00:11.053 --> 00:00:12.234
- Yes, may I help you?

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:13.150
- [Narrator] Are you the person that wrote

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.826
solitaire for Microsoft?

00:00:14.826 --> 00:00:17.347
- Yes, but that was a long time ago.

00:00:17.347 --> 00:00:18.180
- [Narrator] We gotta talk.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.013
Can we come in?

00:00:19.013 --> 00:00:19.989
Great, thank you.

00:00:19.989 --> 00:00:22.739
(cheerful music)

00:00:25.764 --> 00:00:26.884
('80's pop music)

00:00:26.884 --> 00:00:28.939
- [Narrator] Alright, this is Wes.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:32.174
- I'm Wes Cherry, and I
wrote Windows Solitaire.

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:35.491
I wrote the game in 1988 as an intern.

00:00:35.491 --> 00:00:37.442
Internships at Microsoft were really an all

00:00:37.442 --> 00:00:39.556
encompassing thing, but
I squoze it in there

00:00:39.556 --> 00:00:41.867
in the few little hours of my free time.

00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:43.021
- [Narrator] For those
who need a refresher,

00:00:43.021 --> 00:00:46.106
Solitaire was played on
the computer using a mouse.

00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:48.097
Cards are pulled from
the deck in an effort

00:00:48.097 --> 00:00:50.575
to stack cards in order by suit.

00:00:50.575 --> 00:00:53.417
And if you won, you got an
awesome display of cards

00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:55.250
cascading towards you.

00:00:56.145 --> 00:00:57.206
- [Wes] I came up with the idea to write

00:00:57.206 --> 00:01:00.844
Solitaire for Windows
out of boredom, really.

00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:02.005
There wasn't many games right at the time

00:01:02.005 --> 00:01:02.869
so we had to make them.

00:01:02.869 --> 00:01:06.554
In the mid to late ’90's was
just when people started

00:01:06.554 --> 00:01:08.390
really getting computers in their home.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.152
Microsoft officially said
that Solitaire was there to

00:01:11.152 --> 00:01:13.466
teach people how to use the mouse.

00:01:13.466 --> 00:01:14.620
But, in reality it was just really just

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.010
something to have fun with.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:17.927
Before they ever released Windows,

00:01:17.927 --> 00:01:20.287
of course Bill Gates got a shot at it.

00:01:20.287 --> 00:01:21.423
His biggest complaint was that Solitaire

00:01:21.423 --> 00:01:22.970
was too hard to win.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.282
- [Narrator] And once
people figured out the mouse

00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:26.252
it became a favorite past time.

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.077
- The number of hours wasted on Solitaire

00:01:28.077 --> 00:01:30.089
is really countless.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:34.391
I can say that right after
Solitaire was released in 1990

00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:35.957
there was a world recession.

00:01:35.957 --> 00:01:37.440
- [Narrator] So many people
were playing it in the workplace

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.954
that Wes came up with what
he called “the boss key.”

00:01:40.954 --> 00:01:43.667
- Press the special key and
a spreadsheet was come up,

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.007
so it made it look like
you were doing real work.

00:01:46.007 --> 00:01:47.986
But, Microsoft made my take it out.

00:01:47.986 --> 00:01:48.819
- [Narrator] Considering this game

00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:49.798
has been around for decades,

00:01:49.798 --> 00:01:51.964
you must have made a ton of money.

00:01:51.964 --> 00:01:54.262
- I was not paid not a single cent.

00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:57.163
At one time I said if I
only got a penny per copy

00:01:57.163 --> 00:01:58.928
I would be very rich.

00:01:58.928 --> 00:02:01.865
So far, only 14 people
have made good on that.

00:02:01.865 --> 00:02:04.363
I'm still waiting for the rest of you.

00:02:04.363 --> 00:02:05.828
- [Narrator] Well, you heard the man.

00:02:05.828 --> 00:02:07.207
Do you still work with computers?

00:02:07.207 --> 00:02:09.047
- I work with Apple now.

00:02:09.047 --> 00:02:10.987
Apples, actually.

00:02:10.987 --> 00:02:12.337
And I own a cidery.

00:02:12.337 --> 00:02:14.075
(crunching)

00:02:14.075 --> 00:02:15.688
- [Narrator] Ah, Wes,

00:02:15.688 --> 00:02:17.362
there's something behind you.

00:02:17.362 --> 00:02:18.595
Wes, run!

00:02:18.595 --> 00:02:20.928
(pounding)

